.TH TKNOTEBOOK 2
.SH NAME
tknotebook \- tabs above a page, out of the widgets there are
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "tk.m";
include "tknotebook.m";
tknotebook := load Tknotebook Tknotebook->PATH;

Notebook: adt {
    top:   ref Tk->Toplevel;
    path:  string;
    tabs:  array of string;
    sel:   int;

    page:   fn(nb: self ref Notebook, i: int): string;
    select: fn(nb: self ref Notebook, i: int);
    chosen: fn(nb: self ref Notebook): int;
};

init: fn();
new:  fn(top: ref Tk->Toplevel, path: string,
         tabs: array of string): ref Notebook;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B New
makes a notebook at
.I path
inside an existing toplevel: a row of tabs above a page.
The caller packs
.I path
itself and packs each page's contents into
.BR page ( i ).
.B Select
shows one page and hides the rest;
.B chosen
says which is showing.
.PP
This
.B Tk
is 8.0-era.
There is no treeview, progressbar or spinbox, and no themed-widget
layer.
The expensive half of that gap wants C; the cheap half is compositions
like this one \(em a frame of tabs, a frame per page, and
.B "pack forget"
for the pages that are not showing.
.PP
This page used to say there was no combobox either, and that was wrong:
.IR choicebutton (9)
is one, showing a value from a
.B -values
list and posting a menu of them when clicked.
It was overlooked because it is not called a combobox.
.SS Why the tabs are radiobuttons
Not a detail of appearance.
A radiobutton is a control with a state, so
.B libtk
already announces it to
.IR voice (3)
when the keyboard arrives at it and again when it changes \(em
.B "General radio button selected"
\(em and a person choosing a tab by ear is told what they chose, without
this module knowing that speech exists.
Buttons would have been silent.
